   419	**Exit.** The exit procedure is not part of this instrument; it is in the operations runbook. No V1 verifier rule
   420	consults any end-of-issuance statement (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-12).
   421	
   422	**The two product guarantees, stated conditionally (CODEX-REVIEW 7).** Guarantee 2 is the C4 window rule, stated
   423	as a property; a finding against it is a C4 finding for the closure rule (A9.2). Guarantee 1 is not computed.
   424	1. **Past receipts survive later compromise.** A receipt whose exact signatures are anchored by T cannot have
   425	   signatures manufactured after an adversary gains signing capability for every required slot substituted into
   426	   it, while SHA-256, the OTS commitment and the pinned chain history stay secure. Its historical authenticity also
   427	   needs the signing and key-use assumptions to have held before T, authentic historical authority, and no relevant
   428	   compromise before T; V1 does not evaluate these (`historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`). A later key theft or
   429	   algorithm break does not by itself invalidate such a receipt. It does not cure earlier theft, unauthorized
   430	   signing before T, misissuance or whole-author-sphere capture, and does not prove that the issuer itself obtained
   431	   the stamp or issued the bundle uniquely.
   432	2. **Signing capability mints only inside the window, in chain time.** Even signing capability for every required
   433	   slot cannot make a receipt pass V1 with verified T after its authenticated not-after, under continuing anchor
   434	   and history assumptions and provided the authority channels are not also captured (republishing a window is a
   435	   new authority event; whole capture is R-2.15). Translating this into a finite UTC cutoff requires an additional,
   436	   explicit backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish: A2.1 registers no finite bound on B − C, and
   437	   neither ε nor S is one. Inside the window, such an adversary mints receipts that pass; the window cannot be
   438	   shortened, and detecting misuse is the B(ii) add-on, not V1.
   439	
   440	**C3 non-claim.** The two keys are *separate technical failure domains under a single controller; no separation of
   441	control or independent fates.* The `gpg` signer signs frames delivered by the AWS-side issuer, so capture of the
   442	issuing account confers signing capability for both slots; the non-exportable key prevents key export, not misuse.
   443	With the hybrid the slots differ in algorithm (lattice and elliptic curve) and custody (AWS KMS and the
   444	slot-2 custody); under the fallback a P-256 break defeats both slots.
